A delete of a root Untyped leaves it unmarked, so once another thread takes away what is still below it,
it is free while what already became roots lies in its memory: two allocators over one block.

Caught-by: fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-rp2350 fuzz-smp2 fuzz-work qemu-replay
Report: memory that may hold kernel objects overlaps a node not above or below it
Witness: fuzz delete-root-makes-nothing

diff --git a/kernel/cap.c b/kernel/cap.c
--- a/kernel/cap.c
+++ b/kernel/cap.c
@@ -295,9 +295,6 @@ bool cap_delete(struct cap *n, bool preempt)
      * An Untyped makes nothing more, as those already roots lie in its memory
      * while another thread may take away those still below it.
      */
-    if (n->type == CAP_UNTYPED) {
-        untyped_set_going(n);
-    }
     while (cap_child(n) != 0) {
         LOOP_PAID(cap_delete, link, "a child below a root, which becomes a root itself");
         struct cap *c = node(cap_child(n));
